1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,202
Sweetheart, every December,

2
00:00:02,202 --> 00:00:06,362
Americans buy more than thirty million poinsettias.

3
00:00:06,542 --> 00:00:09,009
And most of them are dropping their

4
00:00:09,009 --> 00:00:11,406
leaves and dead by New Year's Day.

5
00:00:11,586 --> 00:00:14,171
People think they just don't have a

6
00:00:14,171 --> 00:00:15,058
green thumb.

7
00:00:15,238 --> 00:00:16,364
That's not it.

8
00:00:16,544 --> 00:00:19,622
Almost every poinsettia dies from the same

9
00:00:19,622 --> 00:00:22,480
seven mistakes, and most of them happen

10
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:24,532
before you even get it home.

11
00:00:24,712 --> 00:00:27,986
My mother kept one poinsettia alive for

12
00:00:27,986 --> 00:00:29,077
eleven years.

13
00:00:29,426 --> 00:00:31,899
So tonight I'm giving you the seven

14
00:00:31,899 --> 00:00:35,360
mistakes that kill a poinsettia before Christmas,

15
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:37,408
and how to keep yours red and

16
00:00:37,408 --> 00:00:38,680
full until spring.

17
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:39,998
And stay with me,

18
00:00:39,998 --> 00:00:42,141
because at the end I'll tell you

19
00:00:42,141 --> 00:00:44,618
my mother's secret for making it turn

20
00:00:44,618 --> 00:00:46,560
red again the next Christmas,

21
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:48,636
with nothing but a closet and a

22
00:00:48,636 --> 00:00:49,239
calendar.

23
00:00:49,588 --> 00:00:52,239
The poinsettia comes from Mexico,

24
00:00:52,239 --> 00:00:54,889
honey, where it grows wild into a

25
00:00:54,889 --> 00:00:56,415
bush ten feet tall.

26
00:00:56,595 --> 00:00:59,199
In Mexico, they call it the flower

27
00:00:59,199 --> 00:01:00,577
of the Holy Night.

28
00:01:00,757 --> 00:01:03,008
There's an old story about a poor

29
00:01:03,008 --> 00:01:05,053
little girl who had no gift to

30
00:01:05,053 --> 00:01:07,440
bring to the Christmas Eve service,

31
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:09,553
so she picked some weeds by the

32
00:01:09,553 --> 00:01:09,894
road.

33
00:01:10,074 --> 00:01:12,426
And when she laid them at the

34
00:01:12,426 --> 00:01:15,102
altar, they burst into red stars.

35
00:01:15,282 --> 00:01:17,271
In a minute I'll tell you how

36
00:01:17,271 --> 00:01:18,985
it got its American name.

37
00:01:19,335 --> 00:01:21,677
It's named after Joel Poinsett,

38
00:01:21,677 --> 00:01:24,547
the first American minister to Mexico,

39
00:01:24,547 --> 00:01:26,812
back in the eighteen twenties.

40
00:01:26,992 --> 00:01:28,440
He was a plant lover,

41
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:30,647
and he sent cuttings home to his

42
00:01:30,647 --> 00:01:32,646
greenhouse in South Carolina.

43
00:01:32,826 --> 00:01:35,594
Then a family of growers in California

44
00:01:35,594 --> 00:01:38,144
turned it into the Christmas plant,

45
00:01:38,144 --> 00:01:40,183
and by the nineteen fifties,

46
00:01:40,183 --> 00:01:42,951
there was a poinsettia on every church

47
00:01:42,951 --> 00:01:45,282
altar and in every bank lobby in

48
00:01:45,282 --> 00:01:45,864
America.

49
00:01:46,044 --> 00:01:49,546
And here's something most people don't know,

50
00:01:49,546 --> 00:01:50,421
sweetheart.

51
00:01:50,601 --> 00:01:53,399
The red parts aren't flowers at all.

52
00:01:53,749 --> 00:01:55,791
The red parts are leaves,

53
00:01:55,791 --> 00:01:56,280
honey.

54
00:01:56,460 --> 00:01:58,690
Special leaves called bracts.

55
00:01:58,870 --> 00:02:01,381
The real flowers are those tiny little

56
00:02:01,381 --> 00:02:04,024
yellow-green buttons in the very center.

57
00:02:04,204 --> 00:02:05,522
And that matters,

58
00:02:05,522 --> 00:02:08,699
because those little buttons tell you how

59
00:02:08,699 --> 00:02:10,172
fresh the plant is.

60
00:02:10,352 --> 00:02:12,754
That's the first thing I'm going to

61
00:02:12,754 --> 00:02:13,440
teach you.

62
00:02:13,620 --> 00:02:16,075
My mother taught me in the grocery

63
00:02:16,075 --> 00:02:17,664
store when I was nine,

64
00:02:17,664 --> 00:02:20,408
and I've never bought a bad poinsettia

65
00:02:20,408 --> 00:02:20,842
since.

66
00:02:21,191 --> 00:02:24,096
My mother's first one came from the

67
00:02:24,096 --> 00:02:25,092
bank, honey.

68
00:02:25,272 --> 00:02:26,952
In nineteen fifty-three,

69
00:02:26,952 --> 00:02:29,612
the First National Bank on Main Street

70
00:02:29,612 --> 00:02:32,552
gave a little poinsettia to every customer

71
00:02:32,552 --> 00:02:35,071
who opened a Christmas club account.

72
00:02:35,251 --> 00:02:38,030
My mother saved fifty cents a week,

73
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:40,174
and she got her poinsettia.

74
00:02:40,354 --> 00:02:42,235
She was so proud of it.

75
00:02:42,415 --> 00:02:44,487
She set it on the radiator,

76
00:02:44,487 --> 00:02:46,789
the warmest spot in the house,

77
00:02:46,789 --> 00:02:48,324
so it would be cozy.

78
00:02:48,504 --> 00:02:50,675
It was dead in six days.

79
00:02:50,855 --> 00:02:52,996
I'll tell you what she learned from

80
00:02:52,996 --> 00:02:54,036
that in a minute.

81
00:02:54,386 --> 00:02:57,082
Mistake number one is buying an old

82
00:02:57,082 --> 00:02:57,544
plant.

83
00:02:57,724 --> 00:03:00,119
Look at those tiny buttons in the

84
00:03:00,119 --> 00:03:00,627
center.

85
00:03:00,807 --> 00:03:03,613
If they're tight and green or yellow,

86
00:03:03,613 --> 00:03:04,751
with no pollen,

87
00:03:04,751 --> 00:03:06,192
the plant is young,

88
00:03:06,192 --> 00:03:08,922
and it'll stay beautiful for months.

89
00:03:09,102 --> 00:03:11,677
If they're open and dusty with yellow

90
00:03:11,677 --> 00:03:13,973
pollen, or if they've fallen off,

91
00:03:13,973 --> 00:03:15,782
that plant is already old,

92
00:03:15,782 --> 00:03:17,869
and it'll drop its leaves in a

93
00:03:17,869 --> 00:03:18,982
couple of weeks.

94
00:03:19,162 --> 00:03:21,206
Also check the soil.

95
00:03:21,386 --> 00:03:24,544
If it's bone dry or soaking wet,

96
00:03:24,544 --> 00:03:25,530
walk away.

97
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:28,853
Mistake number two is the trip home.

98
00:03:29,033 --> 00:03:31,074
Poinsettias are tropical,

99
00:03:31,074 --> 00:03:31,564
honey.

100
00:03:31,744 --> 00:03:33,566
They can't stand the cold.

101
00:03:33,746 --> 00:03:36,927
Even five minutes at below fifty degrees,

102
00:03:36,927 --> 00:03:39,643
walking across a parking lot in the

103
00:03:39,643 --> 00:03:41,815
wind, can damage the leaves.

104
00:03:41,995 --> 00:03:43,988
And you won't see it for a

105
00:03:43,988 --> 00:03:44,677
few days.

106
00:03:44,857 --> 00:03:46,712
Then they all drop at once,

107
00:03:46,712 --> 00:03:49,254
and you think you did something wrong

108
00:03:49,254 --> 00:03:49,803
at home.

109
00:03:49,983 --> 00:03:52,239
Always have the store wrap it in

110
00:03:52,239 --> 00:03:53,931
a paper sleeve or a bag,

111
00:03:53,931 --> 00:03:55,834
and put it in the warm car,

112
00:03:55,834 --> 00:03:56,821
not the trunk.

113
00:03:57,172 --> 00:03:59,984
If Grandma's tips are helping you,

114
00:03:59,984 --> 00:04:02,218
tap the like button for me,

115
00:04:02,218 --> 00:04:03,127
sweetheart.

116
00:04:03,307 --> 00:04:06,674
It helps more people find these videos.

117
00:04:06,854 --> 00:04:09,699
Mistake number three is the foil wrapper.

118
00:04:09,879 --> 00:04:12,630
Those pretty foil sleeves around the pot

119
00:04:12,630 --> 00:04:14,418
hold water like a bathtub.

120
00:04:14,598 --> 00:04:16,256
When you water the plant,

121
00:04:16,256 --> 00:04:18,443
the water runs out the bottom and

122
00:04:18,443 --> 00:04:19,570
sits in the foil,

123
00:04:19,570 --> 00:04:20,763
and the roots rot.

124
00:04:20,943 --> 00:04:23,371
You'll see the leaves turn yellow and

125
00:04:23,371 --> 00:04:23,764
droop.

126
00:04:23,944 --> 00:04:25,986
Poke a few holes in the bottom

127
00:04:25,986 --> 00:04:26,803
of the foil,

128
00:04:26,803 --> 00:04:28,709
or take the pot out to water

129
00:04:28,709 --> 00:04:28,913
it.

130
00:04:29,264 --> 00:04:31,771
Mistake number four is watering on a

131
00:04:31,771 --> 00:04:32,398
schedule.

132
00:04:32,578 --> 00:04:34,490
People water every Sunday,

133
00:04:34,490 --> 00:04:36,990
whether the plant needs it or not.

134
00:04:37,170 --> 00:04:39,714
Poke your finger in the soil every

135
00:04:39,714 --> 00:04:40,537
day or two.

136
00:04:40,717 --> 00:04:43,090
When the top inch feels dry,

137
00:04:43,090 --> 00:04:44,277
water it well,

138
00:04:44,277 --> 00:04:45,294
at the sink,

139
00:04:45,294 --> 00:04:47,753
until it runs out the bottom.

140
00:04:47,933 --> 00:04:50,162
Let it drain completely.

141
00:04:50,342 --> 00:04:51,654
Then put it back.

142
00:04:51,834 --> 00:04:54,916
Too much water kills more poinsettias than

143
00:04:54,916 --> 00:04:55,723
too little.

144
00:04:55,903 --> 00:04:57,634
In a minute I'll tell you the

145
00:04:57,634 --> 00:04:59,485
worst place in the house to put

146
00:04:59,485 --> 00:04:59,665
it.

147
00:05:00,015 --> 00:05:02,372
And don't feed it while it's in

148
00:05:02,372 --> 00:05:03,741
bloom, sweetheart.

149
00:05:03,921 --> 00:05:06,405
People think plant food will help a

150
00:05:06,405 --> 00:05:09,314
drooping poinsettia, but it's not hungry,

151
00:05:09,314 --> 00:05:10,307
it's in bloom.

152
00:05:10,487 --> 00:05:12,971
Feeding it now can burn the roots.

153
00:05:13,151 --> 00:05:15,554
Save the plant food for spring,

154
00:05:15,554 --> 00:05:18,344
when it starts putting out new green

155
00:05:18,344 --> 00:05:18,887
leaves.

156
00:05:19,067 --> 00:05:21,818
Right now, all it needs is water

157
00:05:21,818 --> 00:05:23,022
when it's dry,

158
00:05:23,022 --> 00:05:24,570
light, and warmth.

159
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:25,841
Simple.

160
00:05:26,021 --> 00:05:28,645
Just like a houseguest at Christmas.

161
00:05:28,995 --> 00:05:32,013
Mistake number five is the wrong spot.

162
00:05:32,193 --> 00:05:34,648
The worst places are right by the

163
00:05:34,648 --> 00:05:37,252
front door, where cold air blows in

164
00:05:37,252 --> 00:05:39,856
every time somebody comes and goes,

165
00:05:39,856 --> 00:05:42,162
and right next to a heater vent

166
00:05:42,162 --> 00:05:43,204
or a radiator,

167
00:05:43,204 --> 00:05:44,245
where the hot,

168
00:05:44,245 --> 00:05:45,510
dry air cooks it.

169
00:05:45,690 --> 00:05:47,751
Put it near a sunny window,

170
00:05:47,751 --> 00:05:50,194
but not touching the cold glass.

171
00:05:50,374 --> 00:05:53,767
Poinsettias like the same temperature you do,

172
00:05:53,767 --> 00:05:56,330
between sixty and seventy degrees.

173
00:05:56,510 --> 00:05:58,089
If you're comfortable,

174
00:05:58,089 --> 00:05:59,308
it's comfortable.

175
00:05:59,659 --> 00:06:02,375
Mistake number six is not enough light.

176
00:06:02,555 --> 00:06:05,375
People put their poinsettia on the coffee

177
00:06:05,375 --> 00:06:07,577
table in the middle of the room,

178
00:06:07,577 --> 00:06:09,159
or on the dining table,

179
00:06:09,159 --> 00:06:10,534
far from any window,

180
00:06:10,534 --> 00:06:12,598
because it looks pretty there.

181
00:06:12,778 --> 00:06:13,878
And it does,

182
00:06:13,878 --> 00:06:15,437
for about a week.

183
00:06:15,617 --> 00:06:17,950
Then it starts dropping leaves.

184
00:06:18,130 --> 00:06:20,734
Keep it by a bright window during

185
00:06:20,734 --> 00:06:23,102
the day, at least six hours of

186
00:06:23,102 --> 00:06:23,575
light.

187
00:06:23,755 --> 00:06:25,732
Move it to the table for company

188
00:06:25,732 --> 00:06:26,659
in the evening,

189
00:06:26,659 --> 00:06:28,451
and back to the window in the

190
00:06:28,451 --> 00:06:28,945
morning.

191
00:06:29,295 --> 00:06:31,506
So far, check the center,

192
00:06:31,506 --> 00:06:34,072
keep it warm on the way home,

193
00:06:34,072 --> 00:06:35,310
poke the foil,

194
00:06:35,310 --> 00:06:36,726
the finger test,

195
00:06:36,726 --> 00:06:38,230
away from drafts,

196
00:06:38,230 --> 00:06:39,999
and plenty of light.

197
00:06:40,179 --> 00:06:43,657
Mistake number seven is believing they're deadly

198
00:06:43,657 --> 00:06:44,382
poisonous.

199
00:06:44,562 --> 00:06:45,772
For seventy years,

200
00:06:45,772 --> 00:06:48,460
people threw them out because they heard

201
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:51,216
poinsettias would kill their cat or their

202
00:06:51,216 --> 00:06:51,888
grandbaby.

203
00:06:52,068 --> 00:06:54,237
That's an old wives' tale,

204
00:06:54,237 --> 00:06:54,738
honey.

205
00:06:54,918 --> 00:06:58,622
Studies have shown they're only mildly irritating.

206
00:06:58,802 --> 00:07:01,186
A curious pet might get a tummy

207
00:07:01,186 --> 00:07:03,341
ache, or a child might get a

208
00:07:03,341 --> 00:07:05,187
rash from the milky sap.

209
00:07:05,537 --> 00:07:07,676
Still keep it out of reach of

210
00:07:07,676 --> 00:07:09,446
puppies and little ones,

211
00:07:09,446 --> 00:07:12,914
sweetheart, because nobody wants an upset tummy

212
00:07:12,914 --> 00:07:13,873
at Christmas.

213
00:07:14,053 --> 00:07:16,015
And wash your hands if you get

214
00:07:16,015 --> 00:07:17,454
the white sap on them.

215
00:07:17,634 --> 00:07:21,208
Some people with latex allergies are sensitive

216
00:07:21,208 --> 00:07:21,675
to it.

217
00:07:21,855 --> 00:07:23,805
But you don't need to throw it

218
00:07:23,805 --> 00:07:25,430
away or live without one.

219
00:07:25,610 --> 00:07:27,821
My mother had a poinsettia,

220
00:07:27,821 --> 00:07:29,458
a cat named Biscuit,

221
00:07:29,458 --> 00:07:30,850
and two children,

222
00:07:30,850 --> 00:07:33,552
and all of us survived just fine.

223
00:07:33,902 --> 00:07:35,531
A few more tips,

224
00:07:35,531 --> 00:07:36,142
honey.

225
00:07:36,322 --> 00:07:38,624
If you have a big poinsettia and

226
00:07:38,624 --> 00:07:39,991
it's looking leggy,

227
00:07:39,991 --> 00:07:42,437
group it with two or three smaller

228
00:07:42,437 --> 00:07:43,660
ones in a basket.

229
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:46,147
They hold each other up and look

230
00:07:46,147 --> 00:07:47,084
like one big,

231
00:07:47,084 --> 00:07:48,670
expensive arrangement.

232
00:07:48,850 --> 00:07:50,946
The white and pink ones are just

233
00:07:50,946 --> 00:07:52,716
as easy to keep as the red.

234
00:07:52,896 --> 00:07:54,989
And if you want it to last,

235
00:07:54,989 --> 00:07:56,927
buy it early in December,

236
00:07:56,927 --> 00:07:58,555
not on Christmas Eve,

237
00:07:58,555 --> 00:08:01,346
when the stores are selling the ones

238
00:08:01,346 --> 00:08:03,749
that sat by the drafty door for

239
00:08:03,749 --> 00:08:04,680
three weeks.

240
00:08:05,030 --> 00:08:08,083
After Christmas, keep watering it and keep

241
00:08:08,083 --> 00:08:09,755
it in the sunny window,

242
00:08:09,755 --> 00:08:12,518
and it'll stay pretty well into March.

243
00:08:12,698 --> 00:08:15,567
In spring, when the leaves start to

244
00:08:15,567 --> 00:08:18,271
fade, cut the stems back to about

245
00:08:18,271 --> 00:08:19,583
six inches tall.

246
00:08:19,763 --> 00:08:21,876
In May, put it outside in a

247
00:08:21,876 --> 00:08:23,911
shady spot for the summer,

248
00:08:23,911 --> 00:08:26,181
and feed it once a month with

249
00:08:26,181 --> 00:08:27,042
plant food.

250
00:08:27,222 --> 00:08:30,131
By September, it'll be a big,

251
00:08:30,131 --> 00:08:31,936
bushy green plant.

252
00:08:32,116 --> 00:08:35,529
Bring it inside before the first frost.

253
00:08:35,709 --> 00:08:36,712
Stay with me,

254
00:08:36,712 --> 00:08:38,948
because now comes the secret.

255
00:08:39,299 --> 00:08:41,257
Here's my mother's secret,

256
00:08:41,257 --> 00:08:42,085
sweetheart.

257
00:08:42,265 --> 00:08:45,349
Poinsettias only turn red when the nights

258
00:08:45,349 --> 00:08:46,027
get long.

259
00:08:46,207 --> 00:08:47,375
In the wild,

260
00:08:47,375 --> 00:08:49,713
that happens on its own.

261
00:08:49,893 --> 00:08:50,984
But in a house,

262
00:08:50,984 --> 00:08:53,311
with the lamps on every evening,

263
00:08:53,311 --> 00:08:55,129
it never turns red again.

264
00:08:55,309 --> 00:08:58,127
So starting on the first of October,

265
00:08:58,127 --> 00:09:01,023
every single evening at five o'clock,

266
00:09:01,023 --> 00:09:03,762
my mother put her poinsettia in the

267
00:09:03,762 --> 00:09:04,701
hall closet.

268
00:09:04,881 --> 00:09:06,675
And every morning at eight,

269
00:09:06,675 --> 00:09:08,468
she took it back out to the

270
00:09:08,468 --> 00:09:08,933
window.

271
00:09:09,113 --> 00:09:12,097
Fourteen hours of total darkness every night.

272
00:09:12,446 --> 00:09:14,253
She did it for eight to ten

273
00:09:14,253 --> 00:09:17,063
weeks, honey, with a little calendar taped

274
00:09:17,063 --> 00:09:18,602
inside the closet door,

275
00:09:18,602 --> 00:09:20,074
checking off the days.

276
00:09:20,254 --> 00:09:22,634
Even one night with the light on

277
00:09:22,634 --> 00:09:23,749
could spoil it.

278
00:09:23,929 --> 00:09:26,368
My father teased her and called it

279
00:09:26,368 --> 00:09:28,377
her baby that went to bed at

280
00:09:28,377 --> 00:09:28,735
five.

281
00:09:28,915 --> 00:09:30,045
And every year,

282
00:09:30,045 --> 00:09:33,661
around Thanksgiving, those top leaves started to

283
00:09:33,661 --> 00:09:35,243
blush pink, then red.

284
00:09:35,423 --> 00:09:37,740
By Christmas, it was as red as

285
